Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] drivers: ethernet: w5500: reopen socket 0 when the receive
 state is inconsistent

w5500_rx() takes the frame length from the two-byte header in the chip's
receive buffer and checks it only from below. net_pkt_rx_alloc_with_buffer()
caps the allocation at MTU + Ethernet header for NET_AF_UNSPEC, so a larger
length walks the copy loop past the packet's last fragment and faults on a
NULL net_buf (BFAR 0xc at net_buf_tailroom()). A length of 2 or less is
logged and the function returns, leaving Sn_RX_RD where it was.

On this product the first halts one of two identical boards at every boot,
9 s after start; the board shares SPI2 between the W5500 and an ESP32-C6
without firmware, and in those moments Sn_RX_RSR and the header read as
all-ones (65535, 65533). An earlier version of this patch discarded the
buffered bytes by advancing Sn_RX_RD by Sn_RX_RSR. With a value read in that
state the pointer moved away from the data, every later header read as 0,
and the interface received nothing again until reset (IPv4 and IPv6, link
up; logs in the application repository, docs/device-development/reports/p4/hw).

Now a header of 2 or less, larger than Sn_RX_RSR, or longer than
NET_ETH_MAX_FRAME_SIZE closes socket 0 and opens it again. OPEN resets the
chip's receive pointers; Sn_MR is kept across CLOSE, so MACRAW mode and the
filters stay as configured. Why the reads go wrong is not established here.

Upstream PR #115626 rewrites w5500_rx() and adds a comparison with
Sn_RX_RSR; this patch is dropped when that is merged.
---
--- a/drivers/ethernet/eth_w5500.c
+++ b/drivers/ethernet/eth_w5500.c
@@ -204,6 +204,20 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Socket 0 closed and opened again. OPEN resets the chip's receive pointers,
+ * which is the only way back once the length header and Sn_RX_RD disagree:
+ * advancing the pointer by a length read in that state only moves it further
+ * from the data. Sn_MR survives CLOSE, so the MACRAW mode and filters stay.
+ */
+static void w5500_rx_resync(const struct device *dev, uint16_t header, uint16_t buffered)
+{
+	LOG_ERR("W5500 receive state inconsistent (header %u, %u buffered): socket reopened",
+		header, buffered);
+	w5500_command(dev, S0_CR_CLOSE);
+	w5500_command(dev, S0_CR_OPEN);
+}
+
 static void w5500_rx(const struct device *dev)
 {
 	uint8_t header[2];
@@ -230,8 +244,19 @@
 	if (w5500_readbuf(dev, off, header, 2) < 0) {
 		return;
 	}
-	if (sys_get_be16(header) <= 2U) {
-		LOG_ERR("Invalid W5500 header size %u", sys_get_be16(header));
+	/*
+	 * The length is read from the chip's buffer and nothing else checks it.
+	 * One that cannot be a frame, or is larger than the bytes actually
+	 * buffered, means the read pointer and the data disagree. Returning
+	 * without fixing that leaves the pointer where it is, and every later
+	 * frame is read from the same wrong place; walking a too-large length
+	 * runs past the last fragment net_pkt_rx_alloc_with_buffer() gave us and
+	 * faults on a NULL fragment.
+	 */
+	if (sys_get_be16(header) <= 2U || sys_get_be16(header) > rx_buf_len ||
+	    sys_get_be16(header) - 2U > NET_ETH_MAX_FRAME_SIZE) {
+		eth_stats_update_errors_rx(ctx->iface);
+		w5500_rx_resync(dev, sys_get_be16(header), rx_buf_len);
 		return;
 	}
 	rx_len = sys_get_be16(header) - 2;
